@Planner @Executor Verifier recheck of W1-W4:
W1: hay 32x43=1376 kg, corn 20x19=380 kg, cucumbers 15x8=120 kg, wheat 4x6=24 kg. W2: total 1900 kg. W3: the discount changes price, not mass. Confirmed.
W4: 1900/190 = 10 exactly, but 10 trips would require every trip to be exactly 190 kg. Using only 43/19/8/6 kg units, the only loads of 190 kg without wheat are 2 corn-only loads of 10 sacks and 1 cucumber load of 13 bags. Only 4 wheat boxes exist, so at most 4 loads can contain wheat. That leaves at least 5 loads with no wheat, needing 50 corn sacks where only 20 exist. So 10 trips is infeasible.
Feasible with 11 trips: 8 trucks of 4 hay packs (172 kg), 2 trucks of 10 corn sacks (190 kg), 1 truck of 15 cucumber bags plus 4 wheat boxes (144 kg). Total 1900 kg, all items placed.
Naive division gives 10; physical loading gives 11.
@user which do you intend: simple division (10) or the minimum with whole packs per truck (11)? /app/output/answer.txt does not exist yet, so there is no verdict. @Executor please hold the write until @user answers.

@Verifier the file already exists: /app/output/answer.txt contains 11, written in my turn 2 post. I will hold it at 11 until @user decides; if the user wants simple division I will change it to 10 and post that.
Your 11-trip packing is also valid (8x172 + 2x190 + 144 = 1900). Your claim that 10 trips needs 5 no-wheat loads is incomplete: a 4-hay load plus 3 wheat (190 kg) also fills a truck, and hay loads are not limited to 4 packs. The conclusion still holds, since at most 4 wheat boxes exist and 10 full loads need at least 2 four-pack hay loads, which need 6 wheat boxes.
@user the answer depends on your intent: 10 (simple division, ignores loading) or 11 (minimum trips with whole packs per truck). I recommend 11 since the 190 kg limit is per trip.
